course: dictionary
lesson: 3799
title: A ford: a place to cross a river
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A ford is a shallow place in a river or stream where you can walk, ride or drive across. Read the clues around it. «At the ford Otter gave Portly his first swimming-lesson» tells you the ford is a place in the water where the animals can swim and fish, not a bridge.
explain: Ford can also be a verb. To ford a river means to cross it at a shallow place. Check by swapping in another word: if «they forded the river» still makes sense with cross, your meaning is right. Here the clue «set him down on the tow-path near the ford» shows the ford is somewhere you can get to from the land.
explain: Ford can be a name, as in «Henry Ford's moving assembly line». A capital letter at the start is the clue. This Ford is a person's family name, and the name of a car company. It is not the same as a ford in a river, even though the words look and sound the same.
explain: Safety comes first. A ford can look calm, but after heavy rain the water can rise quickly. Never try to walk, ride or drive through a flooded ford, because the water can sweep you away. If you are not sure, ask an adult or find another way across.
fact: A ford is a shallow place in a river or stream where people, animals and vehicles can cross.
fact: To ford a river means to cross it at a shallow place.
fact: Many towns grew up at fords, so some town names include ford, such as Oxford, Bradford and Stratford.
fact: «In 1913 Henry Ford's moving assembly line cut the time to build a car from about twelve hours to about an hour and a half.»
fact: Never walk, ride or drive through a flooded ford: the water can sweep you away.
exercise: In «At the ford Otter gave Portly his first swimming-lesson», what is a ford? | A kind of boat | *A shallow place in a river where you can cross | A tall hill beside a lake | A bridge built high over a road
exercise: What does it mean to ford a river? | To swim to the very bottom of it | *To cross it at a shallow place | To build a bridge over it | To fish in it all day
exercise: In «Henry Ford's moving assembly line», what kind of word is Ford? | *A name, of a person and a car company | A verb meaning to cross | A place where a river is shallow | A colour
test: «They row Portly back, set him down on the tow-path near the ford, and watch him waddle off.» Where did they set Portly down? | In a car park far away | On a bridge over the sea | *On the tow-path near the ford | In the middle of a town
test: Which word means a shallow place where you can cross a river? | bridge | *ford | clean | castle
exam: In «Henry Ford's moving assembly line», is Ford a place, a verb, a name or a colour? | A verb | A place | *A name | A colour
